##* {{RQ:Raleigh Historie of the World|book=5|chapter=Of the Second Punick War|section=XIIII (The Romans Winne Some Townes back from Hannibal.{{nb...|Hannibal Winnes Tarentum. The Siege of Capua. Two Victories of Hannibal. The Iournie of Hannibal to the Gates of Rome. Capua Taken by the Romans.}})|page=503|passage=This offer vvas contumeliouſly rejected; the ''Capuans'' relying on their ovvne ſtrength, and the ſuccours '''attended''' from ''{{w|Hannibal}}''.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} Of a thing: to be [[in store]] or [[lie in wait]] for (someone or something); to await.
##* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|chapter=Of Power|page=127|passage=To him, I ſay, vvho hath a proſpect of the different State of perfect Happineſs or Miſery that '''attends''' all Men after this Life, depending on their Behaviour here, the meaſures of Good and Evil, that govern his choice, are mightily changed.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[remain#Verb|remain]] and wait; to [[abide]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Robert Abbot (bishop)|Robert Abbot]]|chapter=Of Free Will|title=The Second Part of The Defence of the Reformed Catholicke.{{nb...|Wherein the Religion Established in Our Church of England (for the Points here Handled) is Apparently Iustified by Authoritie of Scripture, and Testimonie of the Auncient Church, against the Vaine Cauillations Collected by Doctor Bishop Seminary Priest, as out of Other Popish Writers, so Specially out of Bellarmine, and Published under the Name of The Marrow and Pith of Many Large Volumes, for the Oppugning thereof.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Impensis}} &#91;[[w:Richard Field (printer)|Richard Field]] for&#93; [[w:Thomas Adams (publisher)|Thomæ Adams]]|year=1607|page=147|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_the-second-part-of-the-d_abbot-robert-bp_1607/page/136/mode/1up|oclc=228716720|passage={{...}} God knocketh at the doore of our hearts, but vvorketh nothing in our hearts, till vve firſt of our ſelues aſſent to let him in. He '''attendeth''' till vve open him the gates, and ''then he vvith his heauenly gifts vvill enter in''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Sterne Sentimental Journey|volume=I|chapter=The Remise. Calais.|page=78|passage=The lady '''attended''' as if ſhe expected I ſhould go on.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|rare}} ''Followed by'' '''[[for#Preposition|for]]''': to expect or look forward.
